.docname {Building from source}
.include {docs}

## Toolchain

The Rust version is pinned in `rust-toolchain.toml` (currently **1.100.0**, with `rustfmt` and `clippy`). Inside the project, rustup picks it automatically. If it is not installed yet, run once:

```shell
rustup toolchain install
```

CI uses the matching `rust:1.100.0-bookworm` image, so a new Rust release cannot change what CI accepts. To move to a newer Rust, change the version in `rust-toolchain.toml` and `.github/workflows/ci.yml` together.

The crate uses the Rust 2024 edition.

## System libraries on Linux

Bevy builds and links against a few system libraries. On Debian and Ubuntu:

```shell
sudo apt-get install pkg-config libwayland-dev libxkbcommon-dev libx11-dev \
  libxcursor-dev libxrandr-dev libxi-dev libgl1-mesa-dev
```

macOS and Windows need nothing beyond the Rust toolchain.

## Building and running

```shell
cargo run             # dev build
cargo run --release   # optimized build, for profiling
```

Dev builds optimize dependencies fully (`opt-level = 3`) and this crate lightly (`opt-level = 1`), because Bevy runs far too slowly unoptimized. The first build is slower as a result, but incremental rebuilds stay fast.

For even faster rebuilds while iterating, link Bevy dynamically:

```shell
cargo run --features bevy/dynamic_linking
```

Release builds use thin LTO and a single codegen unit.

## Web build

The docs site hosts a [web version](try-online.qd) of the app, built from the same code for the `wasm32-unknown-unknown` target. It needs two tools besides the toolchain:

- `wasm-bindgen-cli` at the same version as the `wasm-bindgen` crate in `Cargo.lock`. The build script names the version to install if it does not match.
- `wasm-opt` from [binaryen](https://github.com/WebAssembly/binaryen), which shrinks the module. It is optional for local builds.
- `cargo-about`, which writes the licence notices of the crates compiled in (see [Releasing](releasing.qd#licence-notices)). It is optional for local builds too.

```shell
cargo install wasm-bindgen-cli --version <version> --locked
just web         # builds dist/web
just web-serve   # builds it and serves it at http://localhost:8000
```

`scripts/build-web.sh` builds with the `web-release` profile, which optimizes Bevy for size and this crate for speed, then copies in `web/index.html`, the font's `OFL.txt`, and `THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.html`. The docs workflow runs the same script and publishes the result under `demo/`.

The browser has no file system and no app to quit, so the web build leaves those out. `WEB_BUILD` in `src/shared.rs` hides the file dialogs, file shortcuts, and **Quit**, and skips loading and saving settings; only the native file dialog code is behind `#[cfg(target_arch = "wasm32")]`. Use `bevy::platform::time::Instant` rather than `std::time::Instant`, which panics in the browser.

## Checks

The `justfile` wraps the commands CI runs:

| Command            | Runs                                                           |
|--------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------|
| `just fmt-check`   | `cargo fmt --all -- --check`                                   |
| `just clippy`      | `cargo clippy --all-targets --all-features --locked -- -D warnings` |
| `just check`       | `cargo check --all-targets --locked`                           |
| `just clippy-web`  | Clippy for the web build (`--target wasm32-unknown-unknown`)   |
| `just test`        | `cargo test --all-targets --all-features --locked`             |
| `just bench`       | `cargo bench --bench board_step --locked`                      |
| `just readme-check`| The test that keeps the README's controls list in sync         |
| `just ci-local`    | `fmt-check`, `clippy`, `clippy-web`, and `test`, like CI       |

Run `just ci-local` before pushing.

> Note: The controls list in `README.md` must match the in-app help text exactly; the `readme_lists_every_shortcut_as_help_shows_it` test enforces it. If you change a key binding in `src/shared.rs`, update the README too.

## Continuous integration

`.github/workflows/ci.yml` runs on every pull request, every push to `main`, and every release tag. Pushes to other branches run through their pull request, so nothing runs twice, and a newer push cancels the older run.

| Job              | Runs on        | Command                                                    |
|------------------|----------------|------------------------------------------------------------|
| `fmt`            | Linux          | `cargo fmt --all -- --check`                               |
| `clippy`         | Linux          | `cargo clippy --all-targets --all-features --locked -- -D warnings` |
| `clippy-web`     | Linux          | Clippy for `wasm32-unknown-unknown`, as `just clippy-web`  |
| `clippy-windows` | Linux          | Clippy for `x86_64-pc-windows-gnu`, so Windows-only code is checked before a release |
| `test`           | Linux          | `cargo test --all-targets --all-features --locked`         |
| `test-macos`     | macOS          | The same tests on macOS                                    |

Tags add the release build and release jobs, which wait for all of these; see [Releasing](releasing.qd).

The workflows only read the repository, except the release job, which creates the GitHub release, and the Pages deployment. Every action is pinned to a commit, with its release in a comment; to update one, look up the new release's commit and change both. Lint and test jobs cache their builds, saved only from `main`, as caches saved on other branches can't be restored there. Release builds and the docs site don't use a cache, so nothing a cache holds can reach a published build.

## This documentation

These pages are written in [Quarkdown](https://quarkdown.com) and live in `docs/`. With Quarkdown installed, build the site from that folder:

```shell
cd docs
quarkdown c main.qd --strict     # writes the site to docs/quarkdown-output/gol-bevy/
quarkdown c main.qd -p -w        # live preview while you edit
```

`_setup.qd` holds the shared theme, header, and helpers such as `.keys` and `.card`, and `_nav.qd` the sidebar. A new page needs a `.docname` and `.include {docs}` at the top, and a link from `_nav.qd` or another page, since Quarkdown only compiles pages that are linked.

`.github/workflows/docs.yml` publishes the site to [GitHub Pages](https://andreashdez.github.io/gol_bevy/) once CI passes on a push to `main`, built from the commit CI tested. Pull requests that touch the docs, the app, or the web build build the site too, without publishing it. It builds with `--strict`, so a Quarkdown error fails the build, and uses Quarkdown 2.7.0, pinned in the workflow; use the same version locally, and update both together. It also checks the binaryen download for `wasm-opt` against a pinned hash, so a new binaryen release needs its hash from the release's `.sha256` file.

> Warning: Without `--strict`, a Quarkdown error is only logged and the build still succeeds. A dot directly before a letter starts a function call, even in plain text, so write `\.rle` outside code spans.
